\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93What?\'94 Sarah seethed, stabbing at her holoscreen for any option to block unwanted office calls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s a man here.\'94 The way the receptionist said it left no doubt: he meant a distasteful man. \'93An Agent Jones.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93An agent of what?\'94 Sarah prompted, impatient.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s asking after\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who?\'94 Sarah resolved to get the receptionist's name later and fire him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mr Teh\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Well, shit. The distasteful man could be important and Sarah, on the morning of her first day, felt her insides chill. \'93Could you ask him to make an appointment?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Will do.\'94 The receptionist\'92s holo-head spangled into pixelated stars and receded into the spreadsheets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah ground her teeth as she paged through config menus, looking for ways to prevent the office productivity software from interrupting her. The receptionist\'92s head bubbled back up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What now, dammit? Can\'92t you see I\'92m busy?\'94 she snapped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The man. Jones. He\'92s gone to Mr Teh\'92s office.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Call security,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Aren\'92t you in security?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m in 
\f2\i Cyber
\f1\i0  security!\'94 Sarah spat back. \'93Taxing gonads!\'94 The receptionist\'92s fading expression hovered in faint shock, uncertain if Sarah was cursing him. Sarah slipped on her immaculate shoes with ignoble haste and set off, lady-sprinting down the hall.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah watched the man in Teh\'92s office. Questionably tall and suspiciously fidgety, he went about the office touchy-feeling shelves, opening cupboards, sliding out drawers, examining the undersides of coffee table figurines.  Sarah thought he was square-headed. He had eyebrows like caterpillars. His strangely shimmering red tie seemed at odds with his menace and his black business suit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Can I help you?\'94 she challenged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man looked at Sarah briefly, then resumed his sleazing around the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah held her breath. \'93Well?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He looked at her as if seeing her for the first time. \'93I am to sign some documents,\'94 he grunted, scratching through his black hair in a way that signified threat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you looked behind the painting?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man smiled like a snake. \'93Ah, yes. Very good.\'94 Wagging a finger, he sleazed the painting from the wall, inspected its back, and ran his fingers around the frame. Disappointed, he set the artwork down. \'93I will see the files now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If Sarah\'92s blood was chill before, it became frozen margarita slushing through her veins. She looked to the wider office for security\'97or, failing that, a brave man (Ha! A brave man!)\'97but saw only colleagues gawking over partitions, eyes wide, mouths forming vowels of horror.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What kind of documents?\'94 she asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The kind you have, and I want.\'94 More threat-signalling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mr Teh has retired.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh?\'94 He stopped prowling, fixing his full attention on her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It will take some time to put his outstanding matters in order,\'94 she managed. \'93Perhaps, if you make an appointment\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I will see the files now. Please.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah winced as he advanced, pulling a particle pistol from his jacket. She screamed, only to play her part in the man\'92s expected theatre. Men had waggled weapons at her before, some of them pistols. He blinked, furrowing his brow and cocking his head, as if trying to loosen the reason he hadn\'92t killed her yet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s clear you\'92re not looking for documents,\'94 Sarah said to the suddenly placid giant. \'93Who are you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question seemed to jolt him, as if his train of thought was now running on a completely different gauge. He scowled and blinked, looked down, lifted his foot, and saw his dark shoe-print on the still-moist, freshly steamed carpet. He sniffed the air and caught the perfume of death and cleaning fluids.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mr Teh retired here, in this office,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Thinking she might have the measure of him now, Sarah said, \'93It\'92s not policy to divulge\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There was a cleaner.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes,\'94 Sarah replied, before she\'92d thought much of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He left recently.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes. You didn\'92t see him on your way out?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man chewed on thought. \'93The cleaner has the documents,\'94 he concluded. \'93Did you see the cleaner?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stonewalled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A man? What did he look like?\'94 the man asked, too quietly to be anything but menacing. \'93Who does he work for?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, I don\'92t\'97I doubt\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man advanced on Sarah, then stopped as if struck. His jaw stiffened. His neck bulged. He chewed slowly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The cleaner has the documents.\'94 He pushed Sarah aside as easily as a cardboard cut-out, marched to reception, and took a lift to the building\'92s secure hover-pad floor where his stealth copt was waiting. As soon as he was safe in the starboard couch, its military-grade, over-engineered fans revved up and carried him away.
